WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.890
- [Narrator] Twitter has
been on a rollercoaster

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.280
throughout 2022 ending on October 27th

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.770
in one of the biggest
tech takeovers in history.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.130
Going back earlier this
year here in April,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.890
Elon Musk made a massive 44 billion bid

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.740
for the company but
just three months later,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:17.910
he called it off.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:18.930
Twitter sued.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.150
Musk sued back.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.293
- Oh yeah, Twitter.

00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:26.910
(audience laughing)

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.860
- [Narrator] Then he
changed his mind again.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.810
- This has been a drawn out fight

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.390
between Twitter and Musk
with a lot of twists

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.260
and turns.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.030
- [Narrator] Those twists
and turns take place

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.300
on a timeline that has not
only influenced stock prices

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.360
but has also sown
uncertainty among employees

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.410
and as Twitter says,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.470
contributed to a 1% decline in revenue.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:47.790
So how did Musk's flip flopping

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.920
on the deal lead to a six month battle

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.960
that threw off Twitter's business.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.840
His tweets, statements and text messages,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.453
as well as court documents tell the story.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.230
The timeline starts here

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.050
on January 31st when Musk started buying

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.460
up shares of Twitter

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.140
and he started sharing his opinions

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.390
about how the platform works.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.670
- He did toss out a lot of ideas

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.860
on how to change Twitter,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.140
how he thought he could
make Twitter better.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.660
Things like emphasizing free speech,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:19.920
things like changing

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.410
how it approaches content moderation.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.150
- [Narrator] After months of buying,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.970
Musk publicly disclosed a 9.2% stake

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:27.990
in the company.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.080
His first tweet of the day said simply...

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.700
The next day Twitter announced

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.680
that Musk would be joining the board.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.290
- Musk is one of the most prominent users

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.240
of Twitter but he's also been one

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.620
of its loudest critics.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.540
Twitter invited him to join the board

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.520
to try to bring him into the fold and work

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.500
with him on these issues.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.333
- [Narrator] Musk tweeted

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:48.166
that he was looking forward

00:01:48.166 --> 00:01:49.430
to making significant improvements

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.450
to Twitter in the coming months.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.910
- At first, when he was talking

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.070
with Twitter's leadership,
it was pretty friendly

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.890
but then a friction
tension started to emerge.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.600
- [Narrator] As Musk
was gearing up to join,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.640
he was still criticizing the platform.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.040
Tweeting "Is Twitter dying?"

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.957
CEO Parag Agrawal texted Musk,

00:02:07.957 --> 00:02:09.990
"It's my responsibility to tell you

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.000
that it's not helping
me make Twitter better

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.620
in the current context."

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.947
To which Musk responded,

00:02:14.947 --> 00:02:16.410
"I'm not joining the board.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.880
This is a waste of time.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.340
will make an offer to
take Twitter private."

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.320
- Musk would be the new owner

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.600
and I think the thinking is that

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.970
if Twitter's private he would be able

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.060
to take more risk in trying to overhaul

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.860
or jumpstart their business.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.000
- [Narrator] It didn't take long for Musk

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.440
to act on that promise.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.320
Formerly announcing his
43 billion bid to take

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.960
over the company, the
number was later revised

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.430
to 44 billion.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.170
In an interview with TED that day,

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.270
he explained his decision.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.970
- My strong intuitive sense is

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.880
that having a public platform

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.050
that is maximally trusted

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:58.970
and broadly inclusive
is extremely important

00:03:00.276 --> 00:03:02.310
to the future of civilization.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.840
- [Narrator] On April 25th, Musk

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.840
and Twitter officially
reached a merger agreement.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.390
But even as Musk was
lining up fresh financing

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.820
and cheering more ideas for the platform,

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.530
he had a surprise in store.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.980
- The billionaire CEO
announcing just hours ago

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.880
that the $44 billion deal
to take Twitter private

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.230
is now on hold.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.330
- [Narrator] Musk tweeted
that the deal was temporarily

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.550
on hold as he was waiting
for more information

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.470
from Twitter on the
number of spam accounts

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.550
on the site.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.370
- We know that Musk had talked about bots

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:37.260
before the deal but then he started having

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.690
more concerns or saying
he had more concerns

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.120
and he started questioning
kind of the health

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.200
of Twitter's business.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.550
- [Narrator] Musk expressed interest

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.080
in a lower price for the company

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.050
at the "All-In" Summit on May 16th.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.990
- You can't pay the
same price for something

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.000
that is much worse than they claimed.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.150
- Twitter's CEO addressed
Musk's bot claims,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.550
tweeting in a thread that
the company estimated

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.110
that less than 5% of reported
monetizable daily active users

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.030
are spam accounts and they don't believe

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.300
this specific estimation
can be performed externally.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.130
Musk replied with several tweets

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.250
but one was just...

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.680
Musk continued to push himself further

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.720
from the deal, threatening to walk away

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.840
in a letter to Twitter saying
he believed the company is

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.233
actively resisting and thwarting
his information rights.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.440
Musk's reversal was followed by a flurry

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.300
of coverage and commentary.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.890
- Yep, I feel like Elon is pretty close

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:32.940
to saying, "I'm so sorry.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.860
My two year old took
my iPad and clicked buy

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:36.660
so I didn't know what...
(audience laughing)

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:37.493
So we're cool?

00:04:37.493 --> 00:04:38.640
We're cool, right?"

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.720
- [Narrator] He officially tried

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:40.890
to call the whole thing off

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.510
at the beginning of July.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.680
- I mean, at the start,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.750
Musk was really pushing for this deal

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.000
and Twitter was kind of resisting and then

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.460
it kind of seemed like
their positions changed

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.800
and Musk was the one trying to back out

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:53.820
while Twitter was saying

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.530
that they wanted to close.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.640
- [Narrator] Four days later,

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.020
Twitter formally sued Musk

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.060
in an effort to force the merger through.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.850
- So Twitter said they
did share information

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.970
about how they handled
spam and fake accounts

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.090
with Musk but Musk kept demanding more

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.340
and that was a big part of the legal fight

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.720
that was going on.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.590
- [Narrator] The filing took aim

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.600
at Musk's flip flopping focusing

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.370
on how it affected the company.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.448
- Just looking through the filing here,

00:05:19.448 --> 00:05:22.620
it says quote, Musk
apparently believes that he,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.360
unlike every other party subject

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.040
to Delaware contract law is free

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.500
to change his mind, trash the company,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.800
disrupt its operations,
destroy stockholder value

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.180
and walk away.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.010
- [Narrator] Musk filed a counter suit

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.840
claiming revisions to user numbers

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.310
in the company's first quarter earnings

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.320
in April were what sparked his push

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.730
for bot data.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.130
Twitter rejected Musk's claims.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.500
As both sides prepared
for a fight in court,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.110
a former Twitter employee
filed a whistleblower complaint

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.020
against the company
alleging among other things

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.960
that Twitter doesn't
properly count spam accounts.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.150
Musk seized on that as further ammunition

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.370
to call the agreement off.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.850
But after pushing for
months to end the deal,

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.800
he changed his mind again.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.270
At the beginning of October,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.940
he proposed closing on the original terms.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.040
- That was surprising
because it's not really

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.630
like him to back down.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.030
I mean, we've seen prior lawsuits

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.300
where he's fought more and for longer.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.590
- [Narrator] In a Tesla earnings call

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.260
on October 19th, Musk spoke briefly

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.280
about the deal.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.770
- [Musk] Although obviously myself

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.130
and the other investors
are obviously overpaying

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.653
for Twitter right now,

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.740
the long term potential for Twitter

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.630
in my view is in order
of magnitude greater

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.040
than it's current value.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.240
- [Narrator] Twitter isn't in the shape

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.430
it was back in April.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.040
- We have more economic concerns

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.630
just broadly in the economy.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.040
And then specifically for Twitter,

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.250
the uncertainty has weighed on Twitter

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.170
because no one knew if
the deal was actually

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.730
gonna go through.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.440
- [Narrator] Upon completing the takeover,

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.960
Musk fired CEO Parag Agrawal,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.180
and Chief Financial Officer, Ned Segal,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.460
according to people
familiar with the matter.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.960
Twitter did not immediately respond

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.110
to request for comment.

